Z-Score Walk-Forward Engine
A monthly-recalibrated walk-forward validation framework for a mean-reversion signal on futures data, built around three ideas:
No single train/test split. The strategy re-qualifies its active signal combinations every month using only trailing data, rather than being validated once on a fixed historical window.
Adaptive exits, not fixed ones. For each qualifying signal, the exit point is re-derived monthly from realized bin-transition data — whichever exit zone produced the best mean net P&L over the trailing lookback wins, with a fallback if there isn't enough data yet.
Explicit lookahead-bias verification. Before trusting any result, the notebook re-derives z-scores using only past data at a sample point and confirms it matches the full-series calculation, and confirms calibration never uses trades that closed after the recalibration date.
What's withheld, and why
This is a methodology-only version. The actual signal definitions — which z-score bin combinations qualify for entry, the exact calibration thresholds (lookback window, minimum win rate, minimum trades), and the specific exit zone candidates — are replaced with placeholder values in the config block at the top of the notebook.
Everything downstream of that config block — the calibration loop, the adaptive exit optimizer, the walk-forward driver, the causal verification tests, the performance/Sharpe analysis — is the real, unmodified engine. The config block is the only part that encodes the actual edge, so it's the only part that changes between this version and the live one.
How to read this if you're evaluating it
The interesting parts to look at aren't the specific bin thresholds (those are withheld) — they're:
How the causal-bias tests are structured, and why they're run before any result is reported
How the calibration loop separates "does this combination currently qualify" from "did it qualify historically," so the strategy can adapt without silently overfitting to the whole history at once
How find_optimal_exit scores candidate exits only using trades that actually reached the trailing lookback, rather than assuming a fixed exit
Requirements
pandas, numpy, scipy, plotly. Expects two parquet files (a primary 1-minute price series and a regime-indicator series) — set MNQ_PATH and VX_PATH in the config block to your own data.
